# Auto-log app edits to Slack

Whenever an agent makes code or content changes to this app (the Infinity
Windows app in this repo), it must post a short, **plain-English** summary to
the Slack channel **#infinity-app-changelog** so non-technical stakeholders
(Taylor & Ammon) always have a running record of what changed.

## Channel

- Channel name: `#infinity-app-changelog`
- Channel ID: `C0BJ349BJNR`
- Post via the Slack MCP tool `slack_send_message` (already authenticated).

## When to post

Post **once per task**, right after you open the PR and just before you finish
your turn:

- Post when the PR is **opened** (before merge), and include the PR URL. Make
  clear it is not live yet (e.g. "Not live yet — under review").
- If the change is explicitly "local only" / "don't PR", still post the summary
  and note that it was not shipped.
- Do **not** post for read-only work (questions, exploration, reviews) where no
  files were changed.
- Post only **one** summary per task, not one per file or per commit.

## Write it like a person, not a changelog bot

This is the most important rule. The reader is a **non-technical** person. Your
post must describe, in everyday language, **what we're changing and why it
matters to them**. Lead with the human "what & why". Do **not** mention file
names, functions, or technical terms unless there is truly no plain way to say
it.

Shape (1–3 short sentences):

```
:eyes: *<plain-English title of the change>* — <what it does and why it helps,
in everyday words>. (Not live yet — under review.) PR: <url>
```

GOOD:

```
:eyes: *Making installer sign-in faster* — Right now crew have to re-enter their
PIN too often. This change keeps them signed in for the whole workday so they
can get to their jobs quicker. (Not live yet — under review.)
PR: https://github.com/Infinity-Windows/infinity-windows/pull/123
```

BAD (never write like this):

```
Refactored auth session persistence in App.tsx; bumped maxAge to 7d.
```

More guidance:
- Describe user-visible impact first; skip internal noise (lockfile bumps,
  formatting-only churn) unless that was the whole point of the task.
- Never include secrets, tokens, or credentials in the message.
- If the Slack post fails, do not block the task — mention the failure in your
  final chat summary so it can be retried.

## Also write the PR title & body in this same plain-English style

A GitHub Action posts to the same channel automatically when a PR is opened and
when it merges, but it can only see the **PR title and PR body**. So write both
the way you'd explain the change to Taylor/Ammon:

- **PR title:** a short, plain-English "what changed for users" (not
  commit-speak). e.g. "Keep installers signed in for the whole workday".
- **PR body:** start with 1–3 plain sentences on what changed and why it helps
  users, before any technical detail. The Action posts the title plus the first
  part of the body, so the human summary must come first.
